Key Lessons for Future PE Engagements
1. The audit is the sale. The $15K assessment is not a loss leader. It is the product that
proves your value and creates the internal business case for the larger engagement.
Over-deliver on it. Make it investment-committee ready. The operating partner will use
your document to get budget approval.
2. Lead with the recording, not the slide. Playing that one missed call, the $41K
apartment complex HVAC job that walked to a competitor, was worth more than 30
slides of projected ROI. PE people are data-driven, but they are also human. One
concrete example of money walking out the door hits harder than any financial model.
3. Never fire their people. Redeploy them. The fact that PDS moved those 2 CSRs into
revenue-generating roles instead of recommending layoffs was noticed by David, by the
Greenline CEO, and by the Ridgecrest partners. PE firms are sensitive to workforce
disruption. If you walk in and say 'AI will replace 3 jobs,' you will face resistance. If you
say 'AI will free up 3 people to do higher-value work that grows revenue,' you get buy-in
from everyone: the PE firm, the management team, and the employees themselves.
4. Build the dashboard for the PE firm, not the portfolio company. The Greenline CEO
cares about day-to-day operations. David Kaplan cares about EBITDA. The dashboard
that won the portfolio-wide deal was the one that showed David a single number:
monthly AI-attributable EBITDA contribution. Make the operating partner look good to
the partners, and the operating partner will open every door in the portfolio.
5. Speed is the moat. The Big 4 firms that David originally contacted quoted 6-month
timelines and $300K-$500K per company. PDS delivered a working voice agent in 4
weeks at a fraction of the cost. In PE, time is literally money because of the hold period.
Every month you save them on implementation is a month of additional value creation
before exit.
6. The portfolio multiplier is real. One successful engagement at Greenline turned into 7
assessments and 7 implementations within 8 months. The per-company sales cycle for
companies 2-7 was essentially zero because David was the one making the introduction
and recommending the engagement. You sell once to the operating partner. They sell
for you across the portfolio.